WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:07.656
<v Daniel>You know, if you ever want to check if a luxury watch is real or like a cheap knockoff, you usually hold it up to your ear and you listen for the tick.</v>

2
00:00:07.836 --> 00:00:12.840
<v Maya>Right, yeah. Because a real mechanical watch has that smooth, sweeping heartbeat.</v>

3
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:21.247
<v Daniel>Exactly. A fake has this rigid, visible mechanical stutter. It just gives away the machine, no matter how good the surface looks.</v>

4
00:00:21.627 --> 00:00:22.648
<v Maya>Yeah, it's a great analogy.</v>

5
00:00:23.193 --> 00:00:31.278
<v Daniel>Welcome to a brand new Deep Dive. Today, our mission is to figure out who is really fooling whom in this wild modern age of artificial intelligence.</v>

6
00:00:31.658 --> 00:00:33.299
<v Maya>And it is a fascinating topic.</v>

7
00:00:33.579 --> 00:00:43.905
<v Daniel>It really is. We are going to answer that by unpacking a massive, highly anticipated paper that just dropped on October 26, 2026 in Nature Human Behavior.</v>

8
00:00:44.305 --> 00:00:47.326
<v Maya>Yes, and it is just a monumental piece of research.</v>

9
00:00:47.587 --> 00:00:48.107
<v Daniel>Oh, absolutely.</v>

10
00:00:48.547 --> 00:00:57.890
<v Maya>The paper's titled, Touring-Like Tests for Language and Vision Demonstrate a Narrowing Gap Between Humans and AI. It's led by Memi Zhang out of Nanyang Technological University.</v>

11
00:00:58.171 --> 00:01:01.192
<v Daniel>Along with Pavarino, Liu, and a huge team, right?</v>

12
00:01:01.432 --> 00:01:15.697
<v Maya>Exactly. And the senior author is Gabriel Kreiman from Boston Children's Hospital and Harvard Medical School. Right. They worked alongside 21 other colleagues in total, including heavy hitters like Hans-Peter Pfister, Andre Barbu, and Yisan Ong.</v>

13
00:01:16.048 --> 00:01:21.976
<v Daniel>Okay, let's unpack this because the timeline of this paper is actually like a critical piece of the puzzle here.</v>

14
00:01:22.156 --> 00:01:22.697
<v Maya>It really is.</v>

15
00:01:22.797 --> 00:01:29.867
<v Daniel>This work first started circulating as a preprint called Can Machines Imitate Humans? way back in November 2022.</v>

16
00:01:30.656 --> 00:01:32.237
<v Maya>Yeah, quite a while ago in AI years.</v>

17
00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:46.944
<v Daniel>Seriously. And it was officially submitted in August 2024. So why does that matter for you listening? Because the AI models they tested, systems like GPT-3, BlenderBot, BlipJet, up to</v>

18
00:01:47.604 --> 00:01:53.067
<v Daniel>GPT-3.5 Turbo, Lama 3-70B, and ChatGPT-4.0.</v>

19
00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:56.569
<v Maya>Right. They are not this exact week's absolute frontier models.</v>

20
00:01:56.769 --> 00:01:58.570
<v Daniel>Exactly. They aren't the brand new models.</v>

21
00:01:58.908 --> 00:02:11.008
<v Maya>But they provide this incredibly robust concrete foundation for what is arguably the largest empirical human versus machine experiment of its kind. We aren't just talking about a few dozen college students chatting with a bot in a lab.</v>

22
00:02:11.526 --> 00:02:18.369
<v Daniel>Far from it. I mean, for decades, we've talked about the Turing test is this, you know, abstract philosophical thought experiment.</v>

23
00:02:18.569 --> 00:02:19.709
<v Maya>Right. Totally theoretical.</v>

24
00:02:20.009 --> 00:02:29.933
<v Daniel>But this paper drags that concept out of the philosophy department and runs over 72,000 rigorous empirical Turing-like tests on real people.</v>

25
00:02:30.453 --> 00:02:32.514
<v Maya>It's just a staggering amount of data.</v>

26
00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:43.180
<v Daniel>We have this absolute mountain of data comparing human and AI behavior, and we're going to sift through it today. And spoiler alert, the gap between humans and AI is absolutely vanishing.</v>

27
00:02:43.461 --> 00:02:46.464
<v Maya>But in ways that completely defy common sense.</v>

28
00:02:46.968 --> 00:02:54.572
<v Daniel>Totally. So to understand the mind bending conclusions this paper reaches, we first need to look at the sheer scale of the playground the researchers built.</v>

29
00:02:54.752 --> 00:02:57.633
<v Maya>Yeah. They didn't just ask a chat bot some trivia questions.</v>

30
00:02:57.693 --> 00:02:57.853
<v Daniel>No.</v>

31
00:02:58.334 --> 00:03:04.076
<v Maya>They designed what is essentially an everyday digital decathlon across six specific tasks.</v>

32
00:03:04.437 --> 00:03:08.839
<v Daniel>Right. And they split right down the middle. So three language tasks and three vision tasks.</v>

33
00:03:09.099 --> 00:03:09.479
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

34
00:03:09.719 --> 00:03:15.282
<v Daniel>On the language side, you have image captioning, word association and just, you know, open ended conversation.</v>

35
00:03:15.904 --> 00:03:30.817
<v Maya>And on the vision side, the tasks get quite fascinating. They tested color estimation, which is taking a grayscale image and guessing the missing colors. Right. They tested object detection. So drawing bounding boxes around things in a scene. And</v>

36
00:03:30.857 --> 00:03:33.499
<v Maya>they tested eye movement attention prediction.</v>

37
00:03:33.639 --> 00:03:37.842
<v Daniel>Wait, let's pause there for a second. How exactly does an AI predict human eye movement?</v>

38
00:03:38.023 --> 00:03:39.344
<v Maya>It sounds like sci-fi, right? Yeah.</v>

39
00:03:39.604 --> 00:03:41.946
<v Daniel>Are we talking about like a webcam watching someone's face?</v>

40
00:03:42.547 --> 00:03:57.412
<v Maya>No, it's more about predicting visual attention maps. So when a human looks at a photograph, our eyes naturally dart to specific things. Faces, high contrast areas, or text. Okay. The researchers took maps of where humans actually looked and then asked vision</v>

41
00:03:57.452 --> 00:04:06.755
<v Maya>models to generate their own heat map of where they think a human would look. Oh, wow. Yeah. Then they asked judges to look at both heat maps and guess which one was made by a real human brain.</v>

42
00:04:07.104 --> 00:04:15.812
<v Daniel>That is wild. Okay, so they gathered answers for these six tasks from 636 real humans and 37 different AI agents.</v>

43
00:04:16.192 --> 00:04:17.213
<v Maya>That's a lot of agents.</v>

44
00:04:17.413 --> 00:04:26.101
<v Daniel>It is. Then they took all those answers, literally tens of thousands of them, and handed them over to 1,916 human judges.</v>

45
00:04:26.521 --> 00:04:33.267
<v Maya>Right, and these judges were recruited across Amazon Mechanical Turk, Prolific, and even Physical in Lab Testing.</v>

46
00:04:33.632 --> 00:04:38.754
<v Daniel>Just to cover all the bases. Plus, they brought in 10 AI judges, which we will definitely get back to.</v>

47
00:04:38.954 --> 00:04:39.514
<v Maya>Oh, absolutely.</v>

48
00:04:40.154 --> 00:04:46.356
<v Daniel>The judges had one simple job. Look at an answer and decide, did a human make this or did a machine?</v>

49
00:04:46.957 --> 00:04:53.979
<v Maya>And to measure how well the AI was doing across this massive data set, they used a metric called the imitation detectability score.</v>

50
00:04:54.334 --> 00:05:03.081
<v Daniel>Let me break that score down real quick. It's basically a spectrum that averages how often a judge correctly calls a human human and a machine machine.</v>

51
00:05:03.501 --> 00:05:03.681
<v Maya>Right.</v>

52
00:05:04.182 --> 00:05:08.505
<v Daniel>So a score of 1.0 means the AI is a terrible imitator. It's totally obvious.</v>

53
00:05:08.745 --> 00:05:09.466
<v Maya>Everyone spots it.</v>

54
00:05:09.606 --> 00:05:17.072
<v Daniel>Exactly. But a score of 0.5, that just means the judge is basically flipping a coin. They have absolutely no clue who is who.</v>

55
00:05:17.493 --> 00:05:23.098
<v Maya>And the baseline numbers from this paper show that AI is terrifyingly close to that coin toss.</v>

56
00:05:23.178 --> 00:05:24.159
<v Daniel>Yeah, leave the numbers on us.</v>

57
00:05:24.679 --> 00:05:33.207
<v Maya>So in image captioning, the detectability score was 0.57. Word association was 0.53. Conversation was 0.57.</v>

58
00:05:33.387 --> 00:05:34.948
<v Daniel>That is so close to 0.5.</v>

59
00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:39.332
<v Maya>Right? These machines are hovering right on the edge of perfect deception.</v>

60
00:05:39.967 --> 00:05:44.148
<v Daniel>I think my favorite stat from this baseline data is from an AI to AI setup they ran.</v>

61
00:05:44.368 --> 00:05:45.289
<v Maya>Oh, the Blenderbot one.</v>

62
00:05:45.449 --> 00:05:56.392
<v Daniel>Yes. They used Blenderbot, which is a relatively older conversational model from Meta. In this setup, Blenderbot was actually judged to be human 67% of the time.</v>

63
00:05:56.772 --> 00:05:57.532
<v Maya>Which is incredible.</v>

64
00:05:57.832 --> 00:06:08.656
<v Daniel>It was fooling people more often than a literal coin toss. And ChatGPT 4.0, when they used it as a universal agent across all six tasks, just hung out right around that 0.5 mark.</v>

65
00:06:09.633 --> 00:06:12.534
<v Maya>Yeah, it was bouncing between 0.44 and 0.56. It</v>

66
00:06:13.795 --> 00:06:14.935
<v Daniel>wasn't just language either, right?</v>

67
00:06:15.175 --> 00:06:21.398
<v Maya>No. In the vision tasks, an object detector called Detectron fooled human judges 52% of the time.</v>

68
00:06:21.558 --> 00:06:21.838
<v Daniel>Wow.</v>

69
00:06:22.598 --> 00:06:26.640
<v Maya>And a visual search model called IVSN scored a 0.57. So</v>

70
00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:27.680
<v Daniel>it's across the board.</v>

71
00:06:28.061 --> 00:06:36.484
<v Maya>Yeah, it really is. And if we connect this back to the historical roots, we have to look at Alan Turing's original 1950 paper, Computing Machinery and Intelligence.</v>

72
00:06:36.982 --> 00:06:38.544
<v Daniel>Ah, where the imitation game was born.</v>

73
00:06:38.624 --> 00:06:44.491
<v Maya>Precisely. The authors of this new paper are very careful to call their setup Turing-like tests.</v>

74
00:06:44.672 --> 00:06:45.653
<v Daniel>Right. They emphasize that.</v>

75
00:06:46.394 --> 00:06:54.043
<v Maya>They explicitly state they are not arguing about whether these machines possess true general intelligence or consciousness or a soul.</v>

76
00:06:54.484 --> 00:06:55.325
<v Daniel>They don't care about that here.</v>

77
00:06:55.606 --> 00:07:08.548
<v Maya>Right. They are strictly measuring imitation. Turing's original point was that if a machine can perfectly imitate human behavior, the philosophical question of whether it is truly thinking becomes irrelevant to the person interacting with it.</v>

78
00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.011
<v Daniel>It makes me think of an art forgery. Yeah.</v>

79
00:07:11.211 --> 00:07:11.551
<v Maya>How so?</v>

80
00:07:11.891 --> 00:07:18.814
<v Daniel>Like if someone paints a perfect fake of a Rembrandt. It doesn't mean the forger has Rembrandt's original genius or his emotional depth.</v>

81
00:07:18.954 --> 00:07:19.214
<v Maya>Sure.</v>

82
00:07:19.515 --> 00:07:25.257
<v Daniel>It just means our eyes aren't good enough to spot the brush strokes. The mechanical tick of the watch is completely hidden from us.</v>

83
00:07:25.597 --> 00:07:26.538
<v Maya>That's a great way to frame it.</v>

84
00:07:26.858 --> 00:07:32.020
<v Daniel>But if human eyes and human intuition are failing to spot the fakes, I mean, who can spot them?</v>

85
00:07:32.499 --> 00:07:35.541
<v Maya>And that leads us to the first major revelation of the paper.</v>

86
00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:36.002
<v Daniel>Let's hear it.</v>

87
00:07:36.522 --> 00:07:44.928
<v Maya>Since human judges were basically guessing at random near that 0.5 mark, the researchers threw machine judges at the exact same problem.</v>

88
00:07:45.168 --> 00:07:47.450
<v Daniel>They wanted to see if an algorithm could spot the fakes.</v>

89
00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:51.413
<v Maya>Exactly. And the results completely flipped the script entirely.</v>

90
00:07:52.233 --> 00:07:55.676
<v Daniel>Simple machine judges absolutely crushed the human judges.</v>

91
00:07:55.836 --> 00:07:56.777
<v Maya>Completely crushed them.</v>

92
00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:08.028
<v Daniel>And when I say simple, I mean algorithms that are standard workhorses in the field. They took a linear support vector machine, an SVM, and had it analyze OpenAI text embeddings.</v>

93
00:08:08.328 --> 00:08:08.528
<v Maya>Right.</v>

94
00:08:09.209 --> 00:08:18.395
<v Daniel>This machine judge correctly identified human captions as human 72% of the time and machine captions as machine 83% of the time.</v>

95
00:08:18.856 --> 00:08:21.377
<v Maya>Which is far better than any human judge managed.</v>

96
00:08:22.358 --> 00:08:23.619
<v Daniel>It wasn't just the SVM either.</v>

97
00:08:23.934 --> 00:08:29.657
<v Maya>No, when they used ChatGPT as a zero-shot judge on the vision tasks, it beat the human judges handily too.</v>

98
00:08:29.938 --> 00:08:33.960
<v Daniel>And zero-shot means they didn't fine-tune it or train it for weeks on how to spot fakes, right?</v>

99
00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:43.506
<v Maya>Exactly. They literally just dropped the model into the experiment cold with zero prior examples, asked it to judge the outputs, and it still outperformed human intuition.</v>

100
00:08:44.126 --> 00:08:49.329
<v Daniel>Okay, I'm causing foul on that. What exactly is the machine judge seeing that we aren't?</v>

101
00:08:49.849 --> 00:08:50.530
<v Maya>It's a fair question.</v>

102
00:08:51.431 --> 00:09:06.313
<v Daniel>Because when a human reads a text message to see if it's fake, we look for typos or weird phrasing or like a lack of emotion. If the machine judge isn't looking at basic things like punctuation or word length, how is it doing it? Is it</v>

103
00:09:06.413 --> 00:09:07.675
<v Daniel>reading some hidden metadata?</v>

104
00:09:08.265 --> 00:09:13.007
<v Maya>No, there's no cheating with the metadata here, but it is a wonderfully dull reality.</v>

105
00:09:13.288 --> 00:09:13.548
<v Daniel>Dull?</v>

106
00:09:13.868 --> 00:09:27.955
<v Maya>Yeah. The researchers checked for a simple smoking gun on the surface. They analyzed word length, punctuation patterns, parts of speech frequency. And? They found that no simple surface feature explained the machine judge's success.</v>

107
00:09:29.036 --> 00:09:31.237
<v Daniel>So how does it pull off those massive success rates?</v>

108
00:09:31.837 --> 00:09:37.580
<v Maya>What's fascinating here is that the AI is detecting deep, invisible structural patterns in the embeddings.</v>

109
00:09:38.316 --> 00:09:43.438
<v Daniel>Okay, before you move on, what exactly is an embedding in this context, just in plain English?</v>

110
00:09:43.898 --> 00:09:49.321
<v Maya>Sure. Imagine a massive, multidimensional 3D map of the English language.</v>

111
00:09:49.441 --> 00:09:50.321
<v Daniel>Okay, I'm picturing it.</v>

112
00:09:50.837 --> 00:09:57.161
<v Maya>When a model turns text into an embedding, it doesn't read words like we do. It plots words as coordinates on that map.</v>

113
00:09:57.421 --> 00:09:57.621
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

114
00:09:57.881 --> 00:10:06.007
<v Maya>So the word apple is plotted near fruit and red. Entire sentences become complex geometric shapes connecting these points.</v>

115
00:10:06.267 --> 00:10:06.607
<v Daniel>Oh, wow.</v>

116
00:10:06.827 --> 00:10:14.492
<v Maya>Yeah. So the SVM machine judge isn't reading the sentence for its poetic meaning. It's looking at the math of how those coordinates connect.</v>

117
00:10:14.832 --> 00:10:19.875
<v Daniel>I see. So humans are looking at the painting and trying to judge the emotion of the portrait.</v>

118
00:10:20.035 --> 00:10:20.255
<v Maya>Yes.</v>

119
00:10:20.596 --> 00:10:26.579
<v Daniel>But the machine judge is looking at the chemical composition of the paint itself. It's reading the architecture, not the meaning.</v>

120
00:10:27.039 --> 00:10:36.485
<v Maya>That's a perfect way to look at it. The machine judge can see the mathematical distance between concepts that our human brains simply do not consciously process.</v>

121
00:10:36.725 --> 00:10:37.586
<v Daniel>That is wild.</v>

122
00:10:38.246 --> 00:10:42.569
<v Maya>And this aligns perfectly with the Pagan, Tornberg, Bale, and Berry preprint from early 2026.</v>

123
00:10:43.682 --> 00:10:47.868
<v Daniel>Oh, which you might recall from our previous deep dive, episode 074.</v>

124
00:10:48.268 --> 00:10:50.912
<v Maya>Exactly, the computational Turing test paper.</v>

125
00:10:51.152 --> 00:10:51.553
<v Daniel>Right, right.</v>

126
00:10:52.054 --> 00:10:58.763
<v Maya>Pagan and his team found that classifiers can consistently tell LLM-generated social media text apart from human text.</v>

127
00:10:59.242 --> 00:11:04.664
<v Daniel>Yeah, particularly by picking up on differences in emotional tone and effective expression that humans miss.</v>

128
00:11:04.964 --> 00:11:13.587
<v Maya>Yes. Both the Zhang paper and the Pagan paper point to the same unspedaling conclusion. Machine judges succeed precisely where human intuition fails.</v>

129
00:11:14.127 --> 00:11:19.629
<v Daniel>So the authors predict we are entering a permanent arms race between AI imitators and AI detectors.</v>

130
00:11:20.169 --> 00:11:22.750
<v Maya>Which is incredibly ironic if you think about it.</v>

131
00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:29.503
<v Daniel>we're outsourcing our skepticism. Our absolute best defense against AI deception is actually another AI.</v>

132
00:11:29.943 --> 00:11:30.544
<v Maya>It really is?</v>

133
00:11:31.464 --> 00:11:34.406
<v Daniel>Hold on. There is a massive contradiction in this data.</v>

134
00:11:35.126 --> 00:11:35.546
<v Maya>Let's hear it.</v>

135
00:11:36.066 --> 00:11:46.492
<v Daniel>If machines are so vastly superior at detecting these fakes, why did human judges suddenly achieve a 100% success rate in one specific part of this massive experiment?</v>

136
00:11:46.772 --> 00:11:47.292
<v Maya>Ah, yes.</v>

137
00:11:47.973 --> 00:11:48.773
<v Daniel>That doesn't add up at all.</v>

138
00:11:49.728 --> 00:11:56.831
<v Maya>It makes sense when you look at the rules of engagement. What the researchers discovered is that the format of the test completely dictates the verdict.</v>

139
00:11:57.471 --> 00:11:58.391
<v Daniel>Okay, let's break that down.</v>

140
00:11:58.931 --> 00:11:59.112
<v Maya>Sure.</v>

141
00:11:59.692 --> 00:12:05.994
<v Daniel>In the offline tests, where the human judges were basically hovering around a coin toss, they were just reading finished transcripts of conversations.</v>

142
00:12:06.274 --> 00:12:09.335
<v Maya>Right. It's a passive format. You read a log, you make a guess.</v>

143
00:12:09.956 --> 00:12:13.737
<v Daniel>But the researchers also built a live adversarial app version of the test.</v>

144
00:12:14.038 --> 00:12:28.290
<v Maya>Yes, a real-time chat interface. Imagine you're on a customer service chat right now trying to return a pair of shoes. If you just read the transcript a week later, you might not know if the agent was AI. But if you are live in the chat, you have agency.</v>

145
00:12:29.131 --> 00:12:40.040
<v Maya>In this test, one human judge, one human agent, and one AI agent were all in a room. And the human judge was allowed to actively poke, prod, and interrogate the agents to figure out who was real.</v>

146
00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:45.143
<v Daniel>You can deliberately ask weird off-topic questions. You can use heavy slang.</v>

147
00:12:45.743 --> 00:12:46.144
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

148
00:12:46.704 --> 00:12:53.229
<v Daniel>And when the researchers used GPT-3.5 Turbo in that live app, human judges caught it 100% of the time.</v>

149
00:12:53.549 --> 00:12:53.589
A

150
00:12:53.649 --> 00:12:54.529
<v Maya>perfect strike rate.</v>

151
00:12:55.150 --> 00:13:05.797
<v Daniel>But it wasn't because the AI suddenly got stupid. It was because it couldn't handle being interrogated. It gave itself away through its own built-in safety refusals and polite boilerplate language.</v>

152
00:13:06.233 --> 00:13:11.156
<v Maya>Right. It would fall back on the classic, as an AI language model, I cannot answer that.</v>

153
00:13:11.316 --> 00:13:12.556
<v Daniel>Yeah, totally giving the game away.</v>

154
00:13:13.237 --> 00:13:18.980
<v Maya>Or it would be overly accommodating and polite when a real human would just be annoyed by the interrogation.</v>

155
00:13:19.473 --> 00:13:22.374
<v Daniel>Yeah, the model was completely shackled by its safety training.</v>

156
00:13:22.534 --> 00:13:23.014
<v Maya>Absolutely.</v>

157
00:13:23.314 --> 00:13:29.695
<v Daniel>It's like a method actor. GBT 3.5 is the actor who keeps breaking character to apologize if you curse at them.</v>

158
00:13:29.876 --> 00:13:30.076
<v Maya>Right.</v>

159
00:13:30.476 --> 00:13:37.997
<v Daniel>But the researchers didn't stop there. They took Llama 370B and they intentionally disabled its safeguards.</v>

160
00:13:38.098 --> 00:13:39.058
<v Maya>They took the shackles off.</v>

161
00:13:39.198 --> 00:13:43.439
<v Daniel>They really did. It became the method actor who stays in character even if you throw a chair at them.</v>

162
00:13:43.599 --> 00:13:43.839
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

163
00:13:44.239 --> 00:13:48.180
<v Daniel>And the moment they did that, human judges were no longer perfect. The illusion returned.</v>

164
00:13:48.862 --> 00:14:03.854
<v Maya>As the conversation went on longer, humans got a little bit better at spotting it, but that 100% detection rate completely vanished. Right. And this reveals something deeply profound about how AI deception operates. It actually brings to mind the Jones and Bergen papers from 2024 and</v>

165
00:14:03.874 --> 00:14:18.835
<v Maya>2025. Oh, yeah, the NACL paper. Exactly. In their 2024 NACL paper, Does GPT-4 Pass the Turing Test?, they found that the best GPT-4 prompt only passed about 49.7% of Turing games</v>

166
00:14:18.915 --> 00:14:21.176
<v Maya>against a 66% human baseline.</v>

167
00:14:21.696 --> 00:14:27.260
<v Daniel>Human judges were picking up on style, socio-emotional cues, and that overly helpful tone.</v>

168
00:14:27.831 --> 00:14:31.134
<v Maya>But then in their 2025 preprint, they changed the format, right?</v>

169
00:14:31.174 --> 00:14:45.766
<v Daniel>They did. In their preregistered three-party test in 2025, they gave GPT-4.5 a highly specific persona prompt. Right. They told it to act like a specific human with a specific background, say a 30-year-old accountant from</v>

170
