00:14:55.174 --> 00:15:05.084
<v Daniel>so set that against this zhang papers live test where judges caught gpt 3.5 every single time what does that difference in format and model tell us</v>

175
00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:20.034
<v Maya>It tells us that AI deception is incredibly fragile. If the AI can just sit back and play a prepackaged persona offline, it wins. But the second you put it in a live, unpredictable interrogation where it has to navigate safety guardrails, it shatters.</v>

176
00:15:20.845 --> 00:15:27.311
<v Daniel>It totally does. Oh, and we have to talk about my absolute favorite hilarious detail from the Zhang paper.</v>

177
00:15:27.371 --> 00:15:28.392
<v Maya>Let me guess, the human agents?</v>

178
00:15:28.592 --> 00:15:35.318
<v Daniel>Yes. During the live tests, they explicitly asked some of the human agents to pretend to be an AI.</v>

179
00:15:35.859 --> 00:15:36.079
<v Maya>And?</v>

180
00:15:36.339 --> 00:15:38.561
<v Daniel>And they were caught every single time.</v>

181
00:15:38.781 --> 00:15:39.442
<v Maya>That's amazing.</v>

182
00:15:39.622 --> 00:15:43.385
<v Daniel>We are terrible at faking machine behavior. We just can't help but be human.</v>

183
00:15:43.485 --> 00:15:43.686
<v Maya>Right.</v>

184
00:15:44.517 --> 00:15:57.722
<v Daniel>And what's even crazier is that giving human judges feedback training, literally telling them, hey, you got that last one wrong. Here's why. Or relying on people who claim to have AI expertise. It barely helped them improve at spotting the fakes.</v>

185
00:15:58.103 --> 00:16:02.204
<v Maya>We just do not have the cognitive hardware to spot the structural tells of a language model.</v>

186
00:16:02.384 --> 00:16:06.106
<v Daniel>We really don't. We look for meaning. The machine looks for architecture.</v>

187
00:16:06.404 --> 00:16:19.674
<v Maya>Which leads to a crucial question. Right. If an AI's ability to pass as human relies almost entirely on dropping safety rules or adopting a fake persona, how does the tech industry actually measure what makes a good AI?</v>

188
00:16:20.303 --> 00:16:24.386
<v Daniel>Because they don't use Turing tests for everything. They use massive leaderboards and benchmarks.</v>

189
00:16:24.686 --> 00:16:38.497
<v Maya>Exactly. And this is the final major revelation of the paper. The tech industry relies on standard benchmarks to measure accuracy and skill. Right. But Zhang and her team proved that imitation is fundamentally not the same thing as accuracy.</v>

190
00:16:38.557 --> 00:16:48.106
<v Daniel>Let's explain this because it's wild. The paper shows that how human-like an image captioning model is has almost zero correlation with its standard benchmark scores.</v>

191
00:16:48.166 --> 00:16:48.587
<v Maya>None at all.</v>

192
00:16:48.827 --> 00:16:51.588
<v Daniel>They use things like the blue score or the CIDR score.</v>

193
00:16:51.708 --> 00:16:51.888
<v Maya>Right.</v>

194
00:16:52.328 --> 00:17:01.752
<v Daniel>And a blue score, just to clarify for everyone, is basically a metric that grades an AI by strictly comparing its text against a reference text written by a human.</v>

195
00:17:02.293 --> 00:17:04.514
<v Maya>It's looking for exact word matches.</v>

196
00:17:04.534 --> 00:17:09.456
<v Daniel>Yes, exact matches and engrams. It basically measures how technically correct a caption is.</v>

197
00:17:10.118 --> 00:17:21.244
<v Maya>But the authors argue that passing as human must be treated as a completely separate thing to measure. Being highly accurate and scoring well on a blue test often makes an AI sound decidedly non-human.</v>

198
00:17:21.724 --> 00:17:26.066
<v Daniel>They highlight this vivid example in the paper that just perfectly captures this disconnect.</v>

199
00:17:26.406 --> 00:17:27.227
<v Maya>Oh, the beach photo?</v>

200
00:17:27.367 --> 00:17:36.712
<v Daniel>Yes. They showed an AI and a human the exact same photo. It's a picture of a man sitting on a sandy beach. And a child's head is poking out of the sand right next to him.</v>

201
00:17:36.872 --> 00:17:38.553
<v Maya>Just a funny beach picture.</v>

202
00:17:38.793 --> 00:17:52.152
<v Daniel>Right. And GPT-4, optimizing for accuracy, gives this perfectly articulate description. It notes the man sitting with his back to the camera, the peaceful sky, the child buried up to the neck in the sand.</v>

203
00:17:52.853 --> 00:17:54.013
<v Maya>It is technically flawless.</v>

204
00:17:54.173 --> 00:17:56.054
<v Daniel>Flawless. It would ace a benchmark.</v>

205
00:17:56.735 --> 00:17:57.735
<v Maya>But what did the human say?</v>

206
00:17:58.175 --> 00:18:04.478
<v Daniel>A human looked at that exact same photo and just described it as a headless man holding his own head.</v>

207
00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:06.892
<v Maya>Because of the weird angle of the camera, right?</v>

208
00:18:06.992 --> 00:18:14.480
<v Daniel>Exactly. It looked like an optical illusion. The human brain totally ignored the peaceful sky and the sand and zoomed right in on the joke.</v>

209
00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:15.101
<v Maya>Right.</v>

210
00:18:15.401 --> 00:18:25.392
<v Daniel>So what does this all mean? The AI was perfectly correct. But the human captured the bizarre, striking optical illusion that actually makes the photo interesting.</v>

211
00:18:25.977 --> 00:18:30.880
<v Maya>This is a point Francois Chollet made brilliantly in his 2019 paper on the measure of intelligence.</v>

212
00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:31.760
<v Daniel>Oh, I love that paper.</v>

213
00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:38.504
<v Maya>It's a classic. He explained why the AI field moved away from the Turing test and towards skill-based benchmarks in the first place.</v>

214
00:18:38.864 --> 00:18:41.565
<v Daniel>Right, because benchmarks are measurable and objective.</v>

215
00:18:41.865 --> 00:18:48.329
<v Maya>Exactly. You can easily grade a machine on how well it plays chess or how many items it correctly identifies on a beach.</v>

216
00:18:48.829 --> 00:18:52.432
<v Daniel>But by doing that, we optimize for accuracy, not humanity.</v>

217
00:18:52.512 --> 00:19:07.106
<v Maya>Precisely. Shillay argued that skill benchmarks mask a system's actual ability to generalize like a human does. Yeah. As the Zheng paper confirms today, a model can max out its benchmark scores for image captioning, but still completely fail to</v>

218
00:19:07.146 --> 00:19:11.270
<v Maya>recognize the humorous optical illusion that every single human instantly sees.</v>

219
00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:14.072
<v Daniel>Right. Benchmarks completely miss the essence of human likeness.</v>

220
00:19:14.212 --> 00:19:14.713
<v Maya>They really do.</v>

221
00:19:15.404 --> 00:19:20.850
<v Daniel>Which brings us to the real world. Why does any of this matter outside of a laboratory in Singapore or Boston?</v>

222
00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:22.331
<v Maya>It's a very pressing question.</v>

223
00:19:22.812 --> 00:19:29.658
<v Daniel>The Zhang paper isn't just an academic exercise. They explicitly name the massive practical stakes we are facing right now.</v>

224
00:19:29.999 --> 00:19:36.265
<v Maya>Yeah, we are looking at a future defined by rampant phishing, automated scams, and highly persuasive deep fakes.</v>

225
00:19:37.146 --> 00:19:38.107
<v Daniel>It's already happening.</v>

226
00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:47.473
<v Maya>The authors predict we're entering an era where the default assumption online might be that you are interacting with a machine and you will literally have to prove you are human.</v>

227
00:19:47.873 --> 00:19:52.895
<v Daniel>And that leads to the ultimate dilemma. Do we try to train everyday people to spot the machines?</v>

228
00:19:53.415 --> 00:19:54.956
<v Maya>Well, we saw how that went in the study.</v>

229
00:19:55.356 --> 00:20:04.860
<v Daniel>Exactly. Because the data from all 72,000 of these Turing-like tests shows that human intuition is fundamentally flawed here. We can't see the structural embeddings.</v>

230
00:20:05.140 --> 00:20:05.680
<v Maya>We just can't.</v>

231
00:20:06.285 --> 00:20:14.490
<v Daniel>Or do we hand that crucial job over to machine judges? Do we let algorithms become the bouncers of the internet, deciding who's human and who isn't?</v>

232
00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:28.637
<v Maya>It is a profound vulnerability. If we rely on our senses, we will be deceived. But if we rely on machines to protect us from machines, we are placing our trust entirely in the very architecture we are trying to defend against.</v>

233
00:20:29.118 --> 00:20:34.060
<v Daniel>Here's where it gets really interesting, though. I want you to think about that watch analogy from the beginning.</v>

234
00:20:34.340 --> 00:20:35.101
<v Maya>The ticking watch.</v>

235
00:20:35.476 --> 00:20:49.119
<v Daniel>Right. We listen for the tick to spot the fake. But if we eventually hand over the job of human verification entirely to AI judges, because mathematically they are just vastly better at spotting fakes than we are, what happens to us?</v>

236
00:20:49.459 --> 00:20:50.339
<v Maya>That's a scary thought.</v>

237
00:20:51.019 --> 00:20:58.621
<v Daniel>Will we humans subconsciously start altering the way we type, the way we speak, and the way we communicate just to prove to the algorithms that we are real?</v>

